# Docker and Container Gates

This rule contains only mandatory policy. Use the [containers and orchestration skill](file:///Users/Devesh_Padmanabhan/.cursor/agent-engineering-handbook/skills/containers-orchestration/SKILL.md) and its [Docker reference](file:///Users/Devesh_Padmanabhan/.cursor/agent-engineering-handbook/skills/containers-orchestration/references/docker.md) for procedures, examples, Compose patterns, and troubleshooting.

## Reproducible Inputs

- Pin production base images and deployed images by immutable digest. A readable tag may accompany the digest.
- Do not use `latest` or another mutable tag as a production deployment input.
- Use lock files and deterministic package-manager commands. Do not resolve unconstrained dependencies during the image build.
- Keep build context narrow with `.dockerignore`; exclude `.git`, local dependencies, test artifacts not required by the build, credentials, and environment files.
- Never copy secrets into an image or pass secrets through `ARG`, `ENV`, source files, or the build context. Use BuildKit secret mounts or the deployment platform's secret broker.

## Runtime Security

- Run the final image as a specific non-root user.
- Use the smallest runtime image that satisfies compatibility and support requirements.
- Use multi-stage builds when compilers, package managers, headers, or build-time credentials are not required at runtime.
- Do not grant privileged mode, host namespaces, broad capabilities, or writable host paths without a documented, time-bounded exception.
- Prefer a read-only root filesystem and dropped Linux capabilities where the workload supports them.
- Include a meaningful health check when the runtime exposes a health signal; do not add a probe that only proves the process exists.

## Build Cache Contract

- Order instructions by change frequency: stable toolchain and dependency manifests before frequently changing source.
- Copy dependency manifests and lock files before application source, install dependencies, then copy source.
- Treat intentional layers as cache boundaries. Do not collapse unrelated dependency, build, and source work merely to minimize layer count.
- Combine only logically coupled package-manager operations, such as index refresh, install, and cleanup.
- Use BuildKit cache mounts for performance where useful, but require the build to succeed with an empty or garbage-collected cache.
- Do not disable a package manager's cache while mounting that same cache directory.
- Verify cache reuse with repeated BuildKit builds. If Docker or BuildKit is unavailable, report that runtime reuse was not verified.

## Compose and Publishing

- Use the current Compose Specification; do not add the obsolete top-level `version` field.
- Require explicit immutable production image values. Do not default a missing release version to `latest`.
- Keep production credentials out of Compose environment values and committed `.env` files.
- Treat `docker push`, registry promotion, image signing, and provenance publication as remote mutations requiring explicit authorization under [020-agent-audit.mdc](file:///Users/Devesh_Padmanabhan/.cursor/agent-engineering-handbook/rules/020-agent-audit.mdc).
- Use the organization's approved Sigstore/Cosign or Notation workflow. Do not introduce Docker Content Trust or Notary v1 into new workflows.
- Enforce registry vulnerability policy before promotion or deployment.
